WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.052
Two runs both won eighty percent of their
trials.

00:00:03.052 --> 00:00:04.964
One scored eight out of ten.

00:00:04.964 --> 00:00:09.891
The other scored a hundred and ninety six
out of two hundred and forty five.

00:00:10.375 --> 00:00:13.989
Imagine each run had a fixed length
before the first trial.

00:00:13.989 --> 00:00:18.248
Which score is harder for separated
players using an ordinary shared

00:00:18.248 --> 00:00:19.631
plan to produce?

00:00:19.631 --> 00:00:20.715
Make your pick.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.931
Start with ten independent fair deals.

00:00:25.931 --> 00:00:30.263
Players who always answer zero win three
times out of four on average.

00:00:30.263 --> 00:00:31.728
They still get lucky.

00:00:32.125 --> 00:00:35.625
Each bar shows how often they finish with
that many wins.

00:00:35.625 --> 00:00:38.036
The bright bars cover eight or more.

00:00:38.036 --> 00:00:42.410
Together, those results happen about
fifty three percent of the time.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.921
Now give the same players two hundred and
forty five deals.

00:00:46.921 --> 00:00:50.237
Their wins cluster around a hundred and
eighty four.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.829
A hundred and ninety six sits farther
out.

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:56.708
The chance of that many wins or more
falls

00:00:56.708 --> 00:00:59.876
to about three point nine percent under
this model.

00:01:00.375 --> 00:01:04.183
That larger score comes from a real Bell
experiment in Delft.

00:01:04.183 --> 00:01:08.522
In twenty fifteen, researchers measured
electron spins one point

00:01:08.522 --> 00:01:10.199
three kilometers apart.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:14.112
The percentage answers a question about
the shared plan.

00:01:14.112 --> 00:01:18.688
If that plan generated the data, how
often would its score reach this far?

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:22.132
Now imagine quietly deleting losing
trials.

00:01:22.132 --> 00:01:25.590
Keep the same one hundred and ninety six
wins.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:27.676
Delete all forty nine losses.

00:01:27.676 --> 00:01:29.640
What will your dashboard report?

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:34.841
A perfect run, manufactured with a
filter.

00:01:34.841 --> 00:01:38.314
Decide which trials count before reading
their outcomes.

00:01:38.314 --> 00:01:39.990
Keep a record of the rule.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.520
A file can reproduce the tally.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:46.925
To judge the physical claim, read how the
team separated the stations,

00:01:46.925 --> 00:01:50.383
chose the settings, and decided when a
trial began.
